1
00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.869
Welcome to the final lab in this course.

2
00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.905
In this lab, you'll build a complete document intelligence pipeline

3
00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.274
with LandingAI ADE on AWS

4
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:12.779
that combines automated document parsing with a conversational AI agent.

5
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.483
Just a quick note, this lab assumes basic familiarity with AWS.

6
00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:19.085
However, if you're new to AWS,

7
00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:22.088
You can follow along with the explanations to understand

8
00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:24.691
how production-ready document pipelines are built.

9
00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:26.359
All lab files are available in

10
00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:27.994
the reading item that follows this lab.

11
00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.096
Before diving into the implementation,

12
00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.399
let's review the architecture we'll be building.

13
00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:35.402
You already seen this diagram in the previous video,

14
00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.071
but here's how the data flows through the system.

15
00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.573
First, a user uploads a PDF

16
00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:42.776
to the S3 bucket's input folder.

17
00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:45.378
Second, S3 automatically triggers the Lambda function

18
00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.480
when a new file arrives.

19
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.383
Third, Lambda uses LandingAI ADE

20
00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.986
to parse the PDF into structured markdown.

21
00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:54.888
Fourth, the parsed markdown,

22
00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.589
visual grounding data,

23
00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:59.893
and individual chunks are saved to the S3 output folder.

24
00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:02.195
Fifth, Bedrock Knowledge Base

25
00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:04.397
indexes the documents for semantic search.

26
00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:08.768
And last but not least, users ask questions to the Strands agent,

27
00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.870
which has memory to maintain context.

28
00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.872
There are some prerequisites to the lab.

29
00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:14.808
This lab assumes there are an

30
00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.976
S3 bucket with input and output folders,

31
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.679
and a Bedrock knowledge base connected to the

32
00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:22.282
output medical chunks folder of the S3 bucket.

33
00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:25.085
We'll include links on how to create an AWS account,

34
00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:27.754
set up these resources and learn the basics of AWS

35
00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:30.156
if you'd like to try the lab yourself.

36
00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:33.560
Let's start by installing the packages we'll need throughout the lab.

37
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.763
boto3 is the official AWS SDK for Python.

38
00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.567
This lets you interact with AWS services programmatically,

39
00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:42.669
such as creating a Lambda function,

40
00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:45.138
uploading, downloading files from the S3 buckets

41
00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:48.341
from a notebook environment instead of the AWS console.

42
00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:52.946
python-dotenv loads secrets from a .env file.

43
00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:57.450
Pillow annotates PDF pages with highlights for visual grounding.

44
00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.920
PyMuPDF renders PDF pages as images,

45
00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:03.323
and for AWS agent services,

46
00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:07.327
bedrock-agentcore provides memory management for agents

47
00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:11.331
and strands-agents is a framework for building the AI agent.

48
00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.867
With the packages installed, let's load

49
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.336
our environment variables from the .env file.

50
00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:18.838
This file contains sensitive credentials so we

51
00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:20.940
don't want to hardcode in our notebook.

52
00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:24.344
Here is an example .env file you can use as a template.

53
00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:27.514
Now that our environment is configured,

54
00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:28.815
we need to set up connections

55
00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.316
to the AWS services we'll be using.

56
00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:34.921
The boto3 library provides this capability through clients. Here's how it works.

57
00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:39.125
First, we create a boto3 session that manages the AWS credentials.

58
00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.427
From the session, we create a client for

59
00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.530
each AWS service we want to interact with.

60
00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.499
And we'll create clients for the following services.

61
00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:50.937
s3_client uploads PDFs, download outputs, and manage buckets.

62
00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:55.942
lambda_client deploys Lambda functions, update code, and configure triggers.

63
00:02:56.109 --> 00:03:00.446
IAM client creates roles with proper permissions for the Lambda function.

64
00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:05.813
logs client uses CloudWatch logs to monitor Lambda execution and debug.

65
00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:11.186
bedrock_agent_runtime client queries knowledge bases for document search,

66
00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.394
and bedrock_runtime client calls Claude models directly.

67
00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:17.697
With our AWS clients ready, we can now build the complete pipeline.

68
00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:20.200
Here's the roadmap for what we will accomplish.

69
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.204
Part one, setting up the Lambda Function, steps three through five.

70
00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:26.706
We'll set up the Lambda Function in three steps.

71
00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.742
First, package the code by bundling the Python file

72
00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:32.145
and dependencies into a zip folder.

73
00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.847
Second, create a role by defining permissions

74
00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.150
that allow Lambda to access S3

75
00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.152
for downloading and uploading files.

76
00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:43.790
Third, deploy the function by uploading the package to AWS Lambda.

77
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.159
Part two, setting up the trigger. Step six.

78
00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:50.430
Next, we'll configure S3 to automatically invoke our Lambda function

79
00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.399
whenever new files are uploaded to the input folder.

80
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.636
Part three, building the agent. Step 7 through 12.

81
00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.405
Finally, we'll upload medical research papers,

82
00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:02.442
ingest the parsed documents into the Bedrock Knowledge Base,

83
00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:05.411
and build an intelligent agent using Strands Agent.

84
00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:08.648
To keep this notebook focused on the core concepts,

85
00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:12.051
We've created helper functions in lambda_helpers.py.

86
00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:15.455
These functions handle the lower level AWS operations.

87
00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:18.057
I'll explain the logic of each helper function

88
00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:19.859
as we use them throughout the lab.

89
00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:22.462
Step three, creating the deployment package.

90
00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:24.864
So, what is a Lambda deployment package?

91
00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.866
To create an AWS Lambda function,

92
00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:28.768
you need to bundle your source code

93
00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:31.070
and all its dependencies into a zip file.

94
00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:34.540
This package contains everything Lambda needs to run your code.

95
00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.744
Your source code in ade_s3_handler.py

96
00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.946
contains the ADE parsing logic that

97
00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:42.382
executes when the Lambda function is invoked.

98
00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:46.552
and installed dependencies. All pip packages your source code imports.

99
00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.855
And here's what the package structure looks like.

100
00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:52.558
For creating the package, we'll use the create_deployment_package

101
00:04:52.659 --> 00:04:54.560
helper function to build this package.

102
00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.663
And it takes four parameters.

103
00:04:56.863 --> 00:05:00.066
The source_files, the Python files containing our code,

104
00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:03.469
requirements, the pip packages our code depends on,

105
00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.472
output_zip, the name for the output zip file.

106
00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:11.010
and the package directory, a temporary directory for building the package.

107
00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:14.981
Behind the scenes, this helper function creates a temporary directory,

108
00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:17.984
installs packages using pip into that directory,

109
00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:21.187
copies your source code files to the temporary directory,

110
00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:24.590
creates the zip file from everything in that directory,

111
00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.492
and cleans up the temporary directory.

112
00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:29.796
Before we move to the next step, let's understand the code

113
00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:31.798
that runs inside of Lambda.

114
00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:34.300
This diagram shows the complete flow.

115
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.803
Feel free to explore the source code if you want more details.

116
00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:38.838
Here's what happens step by step.

117
00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:40.940
First event is received.

118
00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.344
When a PDF file is uploaded to the S3 input folder,

119
00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:46.646
an S3 event triggers the Lambda function.

120
00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:50.650
Second, the ADE handler function extracts the file key from the event.

121
00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.853
Third, the handler checks that is a PDF file.

122
00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.824
skips the folders and verifies the output doesn't already exist.

123
00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:01.527
Fourth, the PDF is downloaded to Lambda's temporary directory.

124
00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:04.831
Fifth, the PDF is sent to the ADE API,

125
00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:09.836
which parses it and returns markdown text as well as the chunks.

126
00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:11.904
Six, the results are uploaded to

127
00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:14.307
the S3 output folder in three formats.

128
00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.809
A markdown file for the parsed content,

129
00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:20.713
a JSON file containing chunk information for visual grounding,

130
00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:25.318
and individual chunk JSON files for optimized knowledge base indexing.

131
00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:28.321
Let me help you understand what each output file contains.

132
00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:32.125
For example, if you upload input medical

133
00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:34.494
research_paper.pdf

134
00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:36.229
Here's what you have in the

135
00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:38.297
S3 bucket output folder after ADE process.

136
00:06:38.598 --> 00:06:43.369
The markdown is the complete document in readable format containing anchor tags,

137
00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:45.471
linking text to chunk IDs.

138
00:06:45.972 --> 00:06:48.241
Grounding JSON is a single file containing

139
00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:50.476
all chunks with their bounding box coordinates,

140
00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:54.680
as well as other metadata like chunk type and page numbers.

141
00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:58.418
Individual chunk JSON is one file per chunk,

142
00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.953
optimized for vector database ingestion.

143
00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:03.456
Each file is self-contained with text,

144
00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.858
location, and source metadata.

145
00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:08.161
Similar to the previous lab,

146
00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:09.962
since we're implementing a RAG pipeline,

147
00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:13.366
we'll focus on using only output medical chunks.

148
00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:16.169
For Bedrock Knowledge Base indexing

149
00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:18.171
and for generating the annotated images.

150
00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:21.674
Also, we'll generate the embeddings from each individual chunk.

151
00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:24.377
The other folders can be used for different experiments

152
00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:26.179
and downstream use cases.

153
00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:28.381
Step four, creating the IAM Role.

154
00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:30.883
What is an IAM role? Lambda function

155
00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:35.621
run in isolated containers that have no inherent permissions by default.

156
00:07:35.822 --> 00:07:38.324
They can't access any AWS resources.

157
00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.927
An IAM role grants the function permission

158
00:07:40.927 --> 00:07:44.630
to access specific AWS services using temporary credentials.

159
00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:48.835
For creating the role, we'll use the create_or_update_lambda_role

160
00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:50.570
helper function to create a role

161
00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.638
with the permissions our Lambda function needs.

162
00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:55.341
The roles include these permissions.

163
00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:57.777
For S3 permission, s3:GetObject,

164
00:07:57.777 --> 00:08:00.079
s3:PutObject, and s3:HeadObject,

165
00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:02.982
which help read PDFs from the input folder,

166
00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:05.485
write markdown files to the output folder,

167
00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:09.188
and check if output folder already exists, respectively.

168
00:08:09.589 --> 00:08:12.892
For logs, creates a CloudWatch log group for this function,

169
00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:16.963
create log stream, creates a log stream for each execution,

170
00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:18.865
and logs:PutLogEvents

171
00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:21.868
help write log entries for debugging.

172
00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:24.871
Step five, deploying the Lambda function.

173
00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:27.173
Now we have both pieces we need,

174
00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:29.575
the deployment package, which is our code,

175
00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:31.878
and the IAM role, our permissions.

176
00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:36.582
Let's deploy the Lambda function using the deploy_lambda_function helper,

177
00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:40.686
which expects the function name, the location of the zip file,

178
00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.889
and the IAM role for Lambda.

179
00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:44.891
The deployment includes additional important

180
00:08:44.891 --> 00:08:46.893
run configuration options.

181
00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:49.695
The environment variables are configuration values

182
00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:51.397
our code can access at runtime.

183
00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:53.866
Timeout, maximum execution time,

184
00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:56.269
set to 900 seconds or 15 minutes

185
00:08:56.269 --> 00:08:58.671
for processing larger PDFs.

186
00:08:58.671 --> 00:09:00.873
Memory size, amount of RAM allocated.

187
00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.776
We'll set it to 1,024 megabytes.

188
00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.712
Step six, setting up the S3 trigger.

189
00:09:07.213 --> 00:09:08.915
Our Lambda function is deployed,

190
00:09:09.115 --> 00:09:10.816
but it won't automatically run yet.

191
00:09:11.017 --> 00:09:13.419
We need to tell S3 to trigger the Lambda function

192
00:09:13.519 --> 00:09:15.187
whenever new files are uploaded.

193
00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:17.390
S3 can send events to Lambda

194
00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.192
when objects are created, modified, or deleted.

195
00:09:20.593 --> 00:09:22.595
We'll configure it to invoke our function

196
00:09:22.695 --> 00:09:24.697
specifically when files are uploaded to the

197
00:09:24.697 --> 00:09:26.799
input folder.

198
00:09:26.799 --> 00:09:29.302
The setup_s3_trigger helper handles this configuration.

199
00:09:29.702 --> 00:09:32.505
Step seven, uploading documents for processing.

200
00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:34.507
The infrastructure is now ready.

201
00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:36.142
Now let's upload our medical PDF

202
00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:38.010
documents and watch the pipeline in action.

203
00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:42.214
This diagram shows how PDF files flow from your local medical folder

204
00:09:42.214 --> 00:09:44.717
to the S3 input bucket.

205
00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:47.920
The Lambda function, which is triggered automatically,

206
00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:50.823
processes each PDF using ADE

207
00:09:51.123 --> 00:09:53.826
and produces three types of output files.

208
00:09:54.126 --> 00:09:56.629
We'll use the upload_folder_to_s3 helper

209
00:09:56.829 --> 00:09:58.831
to upload our local documents.

210
00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:01.567
While the Lambda function processes our documents,

211
00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:03.769
we can monitor its progress in real time.

212
00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.772
This helper function watches CloudWatch logs

213
00:10:06.973 --> 00:10:08.774
to display the processing status.

214
00:10:08.975 --> 00:10:12.178
Lambda automatically writes logs, we just need to read them.

215
00:10:12.378 --> 00:10:15.181
To stop monitoring, press the escape button

216
00:10:15.181 --> 00:10:16.882
followed by double clicking i.

217
00:10:17.149 --> 00:10:19.251
You have the option to show all output files,

218
00:10:19.251 --> 00:10:21.454
but for the video, we'll just press no.

219
00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:24.890
Step eight, connecting to the Bedrock Knowledge Base.

220
00:10:25.391 --> 00:10:27.793
Our documents are now parsed and stored in S3.

221
00:10:28.094 --> 00:10:30.496
The next step is to make them searchable

222
00:10:30.596 --> 00:10:32.898
by ingesting them into the Bedrock Knowledge Base,

223
00:10:32.999 --> 00:10:35.701
a vector database that enables semantic search.

224
00:10:36.202 --> 00:10:39.972
First, let's verify that our knowledge base is available and properly configured.

225
00:10:40.373 --> 00:10:42.675
We'll use the Bedrock agent client

226
00:10:42.775 --> 00:10:45.077
to list all knowledge bases and their data sources.

227
00:10:45.277 --> 00:10:48.080
Note that the knowledge base was pre-configured

228
00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.583
in the AWS console to point to our S3.

229
00:10:50.583 --> 00:10:53.686
output medical chunks folder as the data source.

230
00:10:53.886 --> 00:10:56.389
Use Amazon Titan for creating vector embeddings

231
00:10:56.389 --> 00:11:00.760
and storing vectors in OpenSearch Serverless for fast similarity search.

232
00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.062
This information is not printed here,

233
00:11:03.062 --> 00:11:04.697
but I wanted to let you know

234
00:11:04.697 --> 00:11:06.565
about the configurations we use in this lab.

235
00:11:07.066 --> 00:11:10.369
Step nine, ingesting documents into the Knowledge Base.

236
00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:14.173
Now let's sync the parsed documents from S3 into the Knowledge Base.

237
00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:16.375
This process is called ingestion.

238
00:11:16.575 --> 00:11:19.278
So what happens during ingestion? First, the knowledge

239
00:11:19.278 --> 00:11:21.881
base reads all new or modified JSON

240
00:11:21.881 --> 00:11:24.450
files from the S3 output medical_chunks folder.

241
00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.286
Second, it creates vector embeddings for each chunk.

242
00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:31.123
Third, these vectors are stored in the database for fast similarity search.

243
00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:33.159
Once the ingestion is complete,

244
00:11:33.259 --> 00:11:35.895
we can query the Knowledge Base with natural language questions,

245
00:11:36.195 --> 00:11:38.664
and it will find the most relevant document section.

246
00:11:38.998 --> 00:11:42.868
The start_ingestion_job API kicks off asynchronous processing.

247
00:11:43.069 --> 00:11:45.271
It immediately returns the job ID and

248
00:11:45.271 --> 00:11:47.473
the actual work happening in the background.

249
00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.243
Step 10: Creating the search tool with Visual Grounding.

250
00:11:51.544 --> 00:11:54.046
With our documents indexed in the knowledge base,

251
00:11:54.146 --> 00:11:56.649
we can now create a search tool for our agent.

252
00:11:56.849 --> 00:12:00.453
But we're going to add something special, LandingAI's Visual Grounding.

253
00:12:00.853 --> 00:12:03.456
The next cell shows the code for our search tool.

254
00:12:03.656 --> 00:12:06.759
Notice that it's decorated with @strands.tool,

255
00:12:06.859 --> 00:12:09.061
which makes it callable by our agent.

256
00:12:09.462 --> 00:12:12.364
The logic in this code is illustrated in this visual diagram.

257
00:12:12.765 --> 00:12:16.335
Let me walk you through the logic. The tool follows this pattern.

258
00:12:16.335 --> 00:12:20.639
When a user submits a query, like what helps with cold symptoms,

259
00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:24.810
First, Bedrock retrieves by querying the knowledge base using hybrid search,

260
00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.613
combining keyword matching and semantic similarity.

261
00:12:27.613 --> 00:12:29.815
Second, for each result,

262
00:12:29.815 --> 00:12:31.684
it will check if this is

263
00:12:31.684 --> 00:12:33.819
a chunk JSON file from medical_chunks folder.

264
00:12:33.819 --> 00:12:36.822
Third, it will parse chunk JSON to get the metadata,

265
00:12:36.822 --> 00:12:40.326
like chunk_id, chunk_type, page, and bounding boxes.

266
00:12:40.693 --> 00:12:44.096
Then, it will dynamically generate a cropped chunked image.

267
00:12:44.296 --> 00:12:48.501
and upload the cropped image to S3 and return a presigned URL.

268
00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:50.903
Last but not least, the agent will

269
00:12:50.903 --> 00:12:53.105
format the response, give you the source,

270
00:12:53.305 --> 00:12:55.875
the chunk ID, page, chunk type,

271
00:12:55.875 --> 00:12:58.911
crop chunked image URL, and the content.

272
00:12:59.278 --> 00:13:02.882
Before creating the agent, let's verify that our search tool works correctly.

273
00:13:03.082 --> 00:13:06.685
This simple test queries the knowledge base and shows the first result.

274
00:13:07.052 --> 00:13:09.255
Let's search common cold symptoms.

275
00:13:10.055 --> 00:13:12.958
You can see that the knowledge base search is working correctly.

276
00:13:13.259 --> 00:13:15.261
We can now print these test results.

277
00:13:15.661 --> 00:13:17.663
And when you click on the presigned URL,

278
00:13:17.863 --> 00:13:20.466
you can see the chunk image that

279
00:13:20.466 --> 00:13:23.068
was dynamically created for traceability and auditability.

280
00:13:23.469 --> 00:13:27.973
This could be incorporated into your RPA system or any downstream application

281
00:13:28.274 --> 00:13:32.077
for heavily regulated organizations and high-risk operations.

282
00:13:32.545 --> 00:13:35.347
Step 11, creating memory for the agent.

283
00:13:36.048 --> 00:13:37.650
Our search tool is working.

284
00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:39.852
Now, let's create memory for our agent

285
00:13:39.952 --> 00:13:41.654
so it can remember conversations

286
00:13:41.754 --> 00:13:43.856
and learn user preferences over time.

287
00:13:44.256 --> 00:13:46.058
AWS Bedrock Agent Core

288
00:13:46.158 --> 00:13:47.960
provides three memory strategies,

289
00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:49.862
each serving a different purpose.

290
00:13:50.062 --> 00:13:51.864
Summary, summarizes past

291
00:13:51.897 --> 00:13:55.734
sessions. User Preference learns user preferences over time,

292
00:13:56.202 --> 00:13:58.404
and Semantic extracts and stores facts.

293
00:13:58.804 --> 00:14:00.906
We'll configure all three strategies

294
00:14:00.906 --> 00:14:03.709
to give our agent comprehensive memory capabilities.

295
00:14:04.076 --> 00:14:06.078
We'll first check whether or not

296
00:14:06.078 --> 00:14:08.080
there are memories created already for the agent.

297
00:14:08.681 --> 00:14:10.883
Otherwise, we'll create a new memory.

298
00:14:11.217 --> 00:14:15.020
After creating the memory, we need to set up a session manager

299
00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:17.523
that organizes information for each conversation.

300
00:14:17.923 --> 00:14:20.326
Each conversation needs two identifiers.

301
00:14:20.526 --> 00:14:22.728
Actor ID, who is using the agent,

302
00:14:22.728 --> 00:14:25.431
which enables personalization across sessions.

303
00:14:25.731 --> 00:14:29.635
The session ID, a unique identifier for this specific conversation.

304
00:14:30.135 --> 00:14:32.137
Step 12, creating the Strands agent.

305
00:14:32.538 --> 00:14:34.740
We now have all the components ready,

306
00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.543
a search tool with visual grounding and memory

307
00:14:37.543 --> 00:14:40.045
for maintaining context across conversations.

308
00:14:40.246 --> 00:14:43.349
Let's bring everything together into a Strands agent.

309
00:14:43.649 --> 00:14:46.352
The agent is configured with the model,

310
00:14:46.452 --> 00:14:49.555
Claude via Bedrock as the underlying LLM.

311
00:14:49.755 --> 00:14:54.755
the system_prompt, which are instructions that define the agent's personality and behavior.

312
00:14:54.994 --> 00:14:57.997
the session_manager, memory for remembering preferences,

313
00:14:58.497 --> 00:15:00.566
historical summaries, and facts.

314
00:15:00.966 --> 00:15:04.570
Tools, the search knowledge base function we created earlier.

315
00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:07.773
Notice how the system prompt explicitly instructs the agent

316
00:15:07.973 --> 00:15:09.775
to include visual grounding information,

317
00:15:10.175 --> 00:15:12.278
like page numbers, location coordinates,

318
00:15:12.878 --> 00:15:15.281
and annotated images in its responses.

319
00:15:15.748 --> 00:15:17.950
Step 13, the interactive chat.

320
00:15:18.651 --> 00:15:20.753
Your medical document agent is now ready.

321
00:15:20.786 --> 00:15:22.855
Let's start an interactive chat session.

322
00:15:22.955 --> 00:15:26.759
Let's start with how effective is Vitamin C for treating colds?

323
00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:30.296
So, here it shows that it used the tool search_knowledge_base

324
00:15:30.496 --> 00:15:33.699
and it returned a list of symptoms and the source of information

325
00:15:33.799 --> 00:15:36.168
where we can find the annotated image.

326
00:15:36.669 --> 00:15:39.872
I will now tell it that I prefer short answers then exit.

327
00:15:42.474 --> 00:15:44.777
Let's run it again and ask the same question.

328
00:15:45.778 --> 00:15:48.280
You can see that it returned more

329
00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.783
concise answers based on my previous preference.

330
00:15:51.283 --> 00:15:54.086
To exit, type exit, quit, or bye.

331
00:15:54.653 --> 00:15:56.855
Congratulations on completing this lab.

332
00:15:56.855 --> 00:15:59.458
You've built a complete, production-ready

333
00:15:59.458 --> 00:16:03.028
document intelligence pipeline that includes these key components.

334
00:16:03.429 --> 00:16:05.030
Automated Document Processing.

335
00:16:05.331 --> 00:16:07.833
Lambda functions that automatically parse PDFs

336
00:16:07.833 --> 00:16:10.936
